# Citation metadata (Citation File Format 1.2.0) for GitHub's "Cite this repository" button
# and for citation managers. Keep it consistent with .zenodo.json: Zenodo prefers
# .zenodo.json when both files exist, so a change to the title, the authors, the licence or the
# keywords here must be repeated there.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
type: software
title: "Chromascope: an open-source, AI-ready LC-MS workbench"
abstract: >-
  Chromascope is a free, open-source LC-MS workbench written in Rust. It opens mzML data,
  draws chromatograms and spectra, integrates peaks, builds calibration curves and runs
  quantification and QC, as a desktop app, a JSON command-line tool, or over the Model Context
  Protocol (MCP) for AI assistants. Results keep their units, settings, software versions and
  source hashes.
authors:
  - family-names: Cseresznye
    given-names: Adam
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8340-2771"
license: GPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/chromascope-ms/chromascope"
url: "https://chromascope-ms.github.io/chromascope/"
version: 0.3.0
# TODO: add date-released (YYYY-MM-DD): the day version 0.3.0 is published as a GitHub release.
# The doi field is left out on purpose. After the first release has been archived on Zenodo, add
# the concept DOI (the one that always points at the latest version):
#   doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX
keywords:
  - mass spectrometry
  - LC-MS
  - chromatography
  - mzML
  - MCP
